# Routing shared by production and staging.
#
# Both Caddyfiles import these. The only thing that differs between the two
# environments is where certificates come from — Let's Encrypt against the real
# hostnames, or Caddy's own CA against the same hostnames pointed at localhost —
# and everything a test could actually catch is in here, so it has to be the
# same bytes in both. Duplicating the body limit or the compression matcher into
# two files is how a staging pass stops predicting production.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin console, and the tenant-facing report links
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(console) {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Caddy does not send HSTS on its own. No `includeSubDomains`: this is one
	# subdomain of texasrenters.com and the directive would speak for all of
	# them, including hosts this server knows nothing about.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"

	reverse_proxy web:5454
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API, mobile app, report photographs, and the technician event stream
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(api) {
	# Skip images: report photographs are already-compressed JPEG, and gzipping
	# them spends CPU to make them very slightly larger.
	@compressible not header Content-Type image/*
	encode @compressible zstd gzip

	# The backend caps a floor-plan upload at 20 MB. This sits just above it so
	# an oversized file is refused by the application, with its own error, rather
	# than by the proxy with a bare 413.
	#
	# Video is not covered by this and does not need to be: recordings upload
	# from the phone straight to Cloudflare Stream and never traverse this proxy.
	request_body {
		max_size 25MB
	}

	# Upgrades pass through untouched — Caddy needs no directive for that, which
	# is the point. The technician event stream is Socket.IO with
	# `transports: ['websocket']` and no polling fallback, so a proxy that drops
	# the Upgrade header does not degrade it, it removes it, while the REST API
	# keeps working and hides the fault.
	#
	# Helmet on the backend sets its own security headers, including the
	# Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy that lets the console embed report
	# photographs, so nothing here rewrites them.
	reverse_proxy backend:3000
}
